    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Conditional aggregation",
      "body": [
        "Put a CASE **inside** an aggregate to count or sum only some rows, several different ways, in one pass:",
        "",
        "```sql",
        "SELECT start_station,",
        "       SUM(CASE WHEN plan = 'member' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS member_rides,",
        "       SUM(CASE WHEN plan = 'casual' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS casual_rides,",
        "       SUM(CASE WHEN plan = 'casual' THEN fare END)     AS casual_revenue",
        "FROM rides",
        "GROUP BY start_station;",
        "```",
        "",
        "This is how reports get **side-by-side columns** (a pivot) instead of many rows.",
        "",
        "PostgreSQL and SQLite also have a tidier spelling: `COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE plan = 'member')`. SQL Server and MySQL don't, so the CASE form is the portable one."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "blanks",
      "prompt": "Complete the query: rides per day, split into member and casual columns.",
      "code": [
        "SELECT substr(started, 1, 10) AS day,",
        "       SUM(__1__ plan = 'member' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS members,",
        "       SUM(CASE WHEN plan = 'casual' THEN 1 __2__ 0 END) AS casuals",
        "FROM rides",
        "GROUP BY __3__;"
      ],
      "blanks": [
        "CASE WHEN",
        "ELSE",
        "day"
      ],
      "bank": [
        "IF",
        "WHERE",
        "plan",
        "THEN"
